1
00:00:00,333 --> 00:00:03,743
Imagine writing a book on clay,
bamboo, or animal skin.

2
00:00:03,994 --> 00:00:07,044
Before paper, writing was heavy,
rare, and expensive.

3
00:00:07,414 --> 00:00:08,804
Clay tablets could crack.

4
00:00:09,105 --> 00:00:10,975
Bamboo strips took up a whole room.

5
00:00:11,251 --> 00:00:14,341
Silk was light,
but only the very rich could afford it.

6
00:00:14,527 --> 00:00:15,777
Around 105,

7
00:00:16,047 --> 00:00:20,717
a Chinese court official named Cai Lun
showed the emperor something new.

8
00:00:20,962 --> 00:00:25,212
He made sheets from tree bark, hemp,
old rags, and even fishing nets.

9
00:00:25,538 --> 00:00:28,328
The pulp was spread thin on
a screen and left to dry.

10
00:00:28,669 --> 00:00:31,959
The result was light, cheap,
and perfect for a brush and ink.

11
00:00:32,229 --> 00:00:34,379
China guarded the secret for centuries.

12
00:00:34,780 --> 00:00:40,150
Tradition says it spread west
after a battle near Talas, in 751.

13
00:00:40,496 --> 00:00:43,886
Captured papermakers then
taught the craft in Samarkand.

14
00:00:44,127 --> 00:00:48,097
Soon, Baghdad had paper mills,
and its libraries began to fill.

15
00:00:48,382 --> 00:00:51,512
By the twelfth century,
paper mills opened in Spain.

16
00:00:51,913 --> 00:00:54,163
Then came Italy,
and then the rest of Europe.

17
00:00:54,469 --> 00:00:56,519
Cheap paper made cheap books possible.

18
00:00:56,814 --> 00:00:59,284
And that is why knowledge
could finally travel.

